all at this first convening

00:00:12.810 --> 00:00:15.053
of the semester in the virtual world.

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:19.280
First, I hope you and your
families are all staying well

00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:22.490
in these unusually challenging times.

00:00:22.490 --> 00:00:25.670
And as per tradition,
I want to acknowledge

00:00:25.670 --> 00:00:28.270
that we are grateful to the Quinnipiac,

00:00:28.270 --> 00:00:30.940
the Wappinger and the Paugussett people

00:00:30.940 --> 00:00:34.630
as we stand on their land, virtually.

00:00:34.630 --> 00:00:37.100
Keeping in the spirit of recognizing,

00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:39.640
I would also like to take
this opportunity to thank

00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:42.560
Richard and Barbara
Franke for the generosity

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:44.220
in establishing the Franke Program

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:45.340
in Science and the Humanities

00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:48.046
at Yale that enables our activities.

00:00:48.046 --> 00:00:53.046
I'm utterly delighted that
they are here this evening.

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:59.580
I think they are on, and they
are joining us from Arizona.

00:00:59.580 --> 00:01:03.030
So here's a brief preview
of coming attractions

00:01:03.030 --> 00:01:04.610
for this semester is activities

00:01:04.610 --> 00:01:08.160
for the Franke Program before
we delve into today's treat.

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:10.190
So please mark your calendars.

00:01:10.190 --> 00:01:14.350
We've sent everyone save the date.

00:01:14.350 --> 00:01:17.266
Dan Esty will be giving a talk

00:01:17.266 --> 00:01:20.730
on October 15th, and then we have

00:01:20.730 --> 00:01:23.850
Kim Stanley Robinson who will be giving

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:26.010
a talk on 18th November.

00:01:26.010 --> 00:01:28.380
And we have Frank Snowden who will

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:31.210
be talking to us on December 2nd.

00:01:31.210 --> 00:01:35.960
So moving on to today's wonderful event.

00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:38.440
I'm absolutely delighted to invite you all

00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:42.416
to first talk of the
season by one of our own,

00:01:42.416 --> 00:01:44.430
a very distinguished faculty member,

00:01:44.430 --> 00:01:46.443
Professor Valerie Hansen.

00:01:46.443 --> 00:01:49.310
Professor Hansen is a
Stanley Woodward Professor

00:01:49.310 --> 00:01:52.870
of History and her research focus has been

00:01:52.870 --> 00:01:56.760
on China before 1600, Chinese religious

00:01:56.760 --> 00:02:00.223
and legal history and the
history of the Silk Road.

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:05.843
She's the acclaimed author of
several books that include,

00:02:06.730 --> 00:02:10.317
previous books that include
"The Silk Road: A New History,"

00:02:10.317 --> 00:02:13.137
"The Open Empire, A
History of China to 1600,"

00:02:13.137 --> 00:02:16.527
"Negotiating Daily Life
in Traditional China,"

00:02:16.527 --> 00:02:18.340
"Changing Gods in Medieval China,"

00:02:18.340 --> 00:02:21.600
and "Voyages in World History."

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:25.053
She has written numerous essays, chapters,

00:02:26.010 --> 00:02:29.270
translated many scholarly
books and journals.

00:02:29.270 --> 00:02:34.270
She is an authority on China before 1600,

00:02:34.377 --> 00:02:37.450
and in particular has really focused

00:02:37.450 --> 00:02:42.450
beyond China in trying
to understand transfer,

00:02:42.587 --> 00:02:47.190
and the history of what
we now call globalization,

00:02:47.190 --> 00:02:48.630
the history of the process,

00:02:48.630 --> 00:02:53.630
and how peoples coming
together have impacted each

00:02:53.890 --> 00:02:57.783
other's ways of thinking
and the view of the world.

00:02:58.620 --> 00:03:01.380
So during the pandemic,
I usually have been

00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:04.130
waking up to BBC Radio Four,

00:03:04.130 --> 00:03:07.870
and a couple of months
ago I heard her voice,

00:03:07.870 --> 00:03:12.310
which I recognized, talking
about her fascinating new book

00:03:12.310 --> 00:03:15.350
and was so utterly
delighted when she accepted

00:03:15.350 --> 00:03:18.690
our invitation to speak
at the Franke Program.

00:03:18.690 --> 00:03:20.727
So she recently published a book called

00:03:20.727 --> 00:03:24.820
"The Year 2000, When Globalization Began,"

00:03:24.820 --> 00:03:27.580
which looks at the impact of travelers

00:03:27.580 --> 00:03:30.300
who visited unfamiliar locales,

00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:32.889
the early adventurers and explorers,

00:03:32.889 --> 00:03:36.836
and opening up all the land and sea routes

00:03:36.836 --> 00:03:40.300
that mark the true
beginning of globalization.

00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:41.580
So she's got to talk to us.

00:03:41.580 --> 00:03:45.610
In this book, she has focused on travelers

00:03:45.610 --> 00:03:48.160
before the so-called age of exploration.

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:52.900
So these are really the
pioneers who in a way,

00:03:52.900 --> 00:03:54.650
joined the world together.

00:03:54.650 --> 00:03:58.420
She, a brief biography before
I cede the floor to her.

00:03:58.420 --> 00:04:01.070
She joined the Yale faculty
as an Assistant Professor

00:04:01.070 --> 00:04:05.000
of History in 1988, and the
breadth of her intellectual

00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:08.640
interest is the course
titles that she teaches

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:10.573
and I urge you to go and take a look.

00:04:12.310 --> 00:04:15.080
She has won many, many honors

00:04:15.080 --> 00:04:19.390
and is recognized for her expertise.

00:04:19.390 --> 00:04:23.800
Her most recent honors
include the 2013 Gustav Ranis

00:04:23.800 --> 00:04:25.870
International Book Prize for the best book

00:04:25.870 --> 00:04:29.490
on an international subject by
a member of the Yale faculty

00:04:29.490 --> 00:04:31.720
and the 2013 International Convention

00:04:31.720 --> 00:04:35.360
of Asia Scholars Book Prize
Reading Committee Accolade

00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:38.540
for the best teaching
tool in the humanities.

00:04:38.540 --> 00:04:40.700
And I'm sure that this book is going

00:04:40.700 --> 00:04:42.610
to be gathering accolades.

00:04:42.610 --> 00:04:44.070
It has just come out.

00:04:44.070 --> 00:04:48.480
So Valerie, here's to you.

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:51.200
Thank you so much for coming once again.

00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:53.120
- Thanks so much for the invitation.

00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:55.220
And it's a strange of course,

00:04:55.220 --> 00:04:57.170
we're all getting used to Zoom talks.

00:04:57.170 --> 00:04:58.390
I'm still not used to them.

00:04:58.390 --> 00:04:59.960
And I hope there are
no technical glitches.

00:04:59.960 --> 00:05:01.710
And if there are, you'll forgive me.

00:05:01.710 --> 00:05:03.830
I just realized as Priya was giving

00:05:03.830 --> 00:05:05.540
me such a generous introduction,

00:05:05.540 --> 00:05:08.190
that I don't have a
slide showing the book.

00:05:08.190 --> 00:05:10.597
So I just want to show the book (laughs).

00:05:12.450 --> 00:05:14.000
So, it exists.

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:17.223
Now I'd like to share my screen,

00:05:17.223 --> 00:05:20.700
and the talk I'm giving tonight is

00:05:20.700 --> 00:05:23.350
going to be a traditional PowerPoint,

00:05:23.350 --> 00:05:26.430
but we'll let's take
more breaks than usual

00:05:27.930 --> 00:05:32.788
because I went to the
Poorvu Center Teaching

00:05:32.788 --> 00:05:35.060
instruction over the summer,

00:05:35.060 --> 00:05:39.055
and one of their pieces of advice was that

00:05:39.055 --> 00:05:41.980
the students would get bored
after about 10 minutes.

00:05:41.980 --> 00:05:46.110
And I figure you're maybe
good for more than 10 minutes,

00:05:46.110 --> 00:05:51.110
but anyway, there'll be a couple
of breaks during the talk.

00:05:51.130 --> 00:05:55.300
And I wanted to start with something

00:05:55.300 --> 00:05:58.710
Priya mentioned actually
about the main claim

00:05:58.710 --> 00:06:02.010
of the book and the talk
I'm giving tonight about,

00:06:02.010 --> 00:06:06.180
or this afternoon, about when
the age of discovery began,

00:06:06.180 --> 00:06:08.700
and that most peoples,
and when I say most people

00:06:08.700 --> 00:06:12.040
most historians answer would
be 1492 and people would talk

00:06:12.040 --> 00:06:15.130
about Columbus and Da Gama and Magellan,

00:06:15.130 --> 00:06:17.740
but the contention of the book

00:06:17.740 --> 00:06:20.440
and of the talk tonight
is that the Vikings,

00:06:20.440 --> 00:06:24.010
the Malayo-Polynesian and the
Chinese were also making long

00:06:24.010 --> 00:06:27.930
distance ocean voyages much
earlier around the year 1000.

00:06:27.930 --> 00:06:32.020
And the reason that this
is important is that

00:06:32.020 --> 00:06:33.815
when the Europeans arrived

00:06:33.815 --> 00:06:36.249
at different places around the world,

00:06:36.249 --> 00:06:40.960
they found societies that
already had sophisticated

00:06:40.960 --> 00:06:43.970
trading relationships with each other.

00:06:43.970 --> 00:06:48.380
And the level of development
in those different

00:06:48.380 --> 00:06:51.080
places helped the Europeans to penetrate

00:06:51.080 --> 00:06:53.279
those existing markets much more quickly

00:06:53.279 --> 00:06:57.043
than if they had arrived at
places with a blank slate.

00:06:58.400 --> 00:07:01.840
And I wanted to start
this as a kind of classic

00:07:01.840 --> 00:07:05.998
that this book by Richard
Evans, "In Defence of History,"

00:07:05.998 --> 00:07:10.300
originally from 1997, a
manual that's often taught

00:07:10.300 --> 00:07:13.690
to first year history graduate students,

00:07:13.690 --> 00:07:15.830
even first year undergraduates

00:07:15.830 --> 00:07:20.830
about that presents definition
of what a historical fact is.

00:07:21.300 --> 00:07:26.300
And Evans explains that
historians use construct arguments

00:07:27.182 --> 00:07:28.900
that are based on evidence

00:07:28.900 --> 00:07:31.250
and the evidence consists
of different facts.

00:07:31.250 --> 00:07:34.190
And so, and he says that a historical fact

00:07:34.190 --> 00:07:36.920
is something that happened in
history and can be verified

00:07:36.920 --> 00:07:40.950
as such through the traces
that history has left behind.

00:07:40.950 --> 00:07:44.644
And historians almost always
focus on written sources

00:07:44.644 --> 00:07:49.284
but even the early historians
like Herodotus and Polybius

00:07:49.284 --> 00:07:53.655
and people outside of Europe,
like Sima Qian in China,

00:07:53.655 --> 00:07:57.500
traveled to see where
the events that occurred

00:07:57.500 --> 00:07:59.680
that they wrote about to talk to people.

00:07:59.680 --> 00:08:04.250
Polybius has a wonderful passage
where he goes, and he looks

00:08:04.250 --> 00:08:06.220
at the snow in the Alps
when he's trying to figure

00:08:06.220 --> 00:08:10.040
out how an Hannibal crossed
the Alps with his elephants.

00:08:10.040 --> 00:08:12.710
And he looks at the, he
goes and visits the Alps

00:08:12.710 --> 00:08:14.880
to understand the conditions of the snow.

00:08:14.880 --> 00:08:18.400
And my talk tonight is
about the unwritten traces

00:08:18.400 --> 00:08:20.010
that history has left behind

00:08:20.010 --> 00:08:22.463
and how historians can use them.

00:08:23.870 --> 00:08:26.850
And I wanted to start by reminding you

00:08:26.850 --> 00:08:31.470
where writing was first
invented around the world

00:08:31.470 --> 00:08:33.926
and the places with the red circles,

00:08:33.926 --> 00:08:36.340
everyone agrees that this is

00:08:36.340 --> 00:08:38.780
the independent invention of writing.

00:08:38.780 --> 00:08:42.700
So among the Maya in
Mexico, modern day Mexico,

00:08:42.700 --> 00:08:45.330
in Mesopotamia, in China,

00:08:45.330 --> 00:08:50.200
and then the case of Egypt
and Harappan India are debated

00:08:50.200 --> 00:08:52.320
whether this was independent origin

00:08:52.320 --> 00:08:55.190
or they were influenced
by their neighbors.

00:08:55.190 --> 00:08:57.400
But you can see that a lot of the world

00:08:57.400 --> 00:09:00.260
is not covered by these circles.

00:09:00.260 --> 00:09:02.860
And if we jump to the year 1000,

00:09:02.860 --> 00:09:05.450
which is what I'm talking about tonight,

00:09:05.450 --> 00:09:10.450
that the there's a lot
of the world that is not,

00:09:10.600 --> 00:09:11.433
we don't know about that.

00:09:11.433 --> 00:09:13.700
There are no written records from,

00:09:13.700 --> 00:09:17.830
and that's where some
new scientific techniques

00:09:17.830 --> 00:09:21.623
have become very important
for telling us about the past.

00:09:23.720 --> 00:09:27.070
So I'm going to start with an example.

00:09:27.070 --> 00:09:29.240
I'm going to talk about
the Americas first,

00:09:29.240 --> 00:09:32.820
where we have some written sources that

00:09:32.820 --> 00:09:36.340
were actually oral
epics that were recorded

00:09:36.340 --> 00:09:38.170
in old Icelandic, old Norse,

00:09:38.170 --> 00:09:40.950
and then confirmed by archaeology.

00:09:40.950 --> 00:09:44.130
And that's where the chemical
residues will come in

00:09:44.130 --> 00:09:46.371
in terms of American trade patterns.

00:09:46.371 --> 00:09:50.820
Then I'll talk about the
Pacific and the voyages

00:09:50.820 --> 00:09:52.730
of the Malayo-Polynesian.

00:09:52.730 --> 00:09:57.500
It's a mouthful, but it's
just a word for the languages

00:09:57.500 --> 00:10:00.710
that is the ancestral
language of what becomes

00:10:00.710 --> 00:10:04.800
modern Malay and monitoring Polynesian.

00:10:04.800 --> 00:10:07.090
And then I'll close with the Chinese,

00:10:07.090 --> 00:10:08.480
which is home territory for me.

00:10:08.480 --> 00:10:10.740
I am a Chinese historian,

00:10:10.740 --> 00:10:13.277
and for China, we have a
lot of written sources.

00:10:13.277 --> 00:10:15.990
And so the archaeological finds are really

00:10:15.990 --> 00:10:18.853
a supplement to those written sources.

00:10:20.027 --> 00:10:23.403
So I begin with the Vikings,

00:10:24.300 --> 00:10:27.760
and these voyages are described

00:10:27.760 --> 00:10:31.665
in the Vinland Sagas, which
were transmitted orally

00:10:31.665 --> 00:10:34.670
for several centuries
before they were written

00:10:34.670 --> 00:10:37.630
down in the 13th and 14th centuries.

00:10:37.630 --> 00:10:41.050
And you can see that the Vikings

00:10:41.050 --> 00:10:44.870
or the Norse traveled from Scandinavia

00:10:44.870 --> 00:10:48.660
to Iceland and from there
actually to Greenland

00:10:48.660 --> 00:10:53.660
and then from Greenland they
go up, this is Baffin Island

00:10:53.930 --> 00:10:58.420
in the Bay of Baffin, and
then travel south and land.

00:10:58.420 --> 00:11:00.250
And we know this because of archeology,

00:11:00.250 --> 00:11:03.510
land on the northern tip of Newfoundland

00:11:03.510 --> 00:11:06.353
at an archaeological site
called L'Anse Aux Meadows.

00:11:08.110 --> 00:11:10.200
So what I was going to say, we know this,

00:11:10.200 --> 00:11:12.290
but when people first read the sagas,

00:11:12.290 --> 00:11:14.900
they didn't know where Vinland was.

00:11:14.900 --> 00:11:19.900
The sagas talk about a
place that was very fertile

00:11:20.560 --> 00:11:23.060
and were wild grapes grew.

00:11:23.060 --> 00:11:25.150
And so most people thought

00:11:25.150 --> 00:11:29.933
that Vinland was actually
farther south than Newfoundland.

00:11:30.970 --> 00:11:34.690
In the middle of the early,
the mid 19th century,

00:11:34.690 --> 00:11:37.520
one scholar proposed
that Martha's Vineyard

00:11:37.520 --> 00:11:39.181
was actually Vinland,

00:11:39.181 --> 00:11:44.003
but in 1960, two Norwegians,

00:11:44.003 --> 00:11:46.680
a diplomat and his archeologist wife

00:11:46.680 --> 00:11:49.190
arrived at L'Anse Aux Meadows.

00:11:49.190 --> 00:11:51.720
And they talk to the locals and asked them

00:11:51.720 --> 00:11:55.880
if there were any places that
might be a Norse settlement.

00:11:55.880 --> 00:12:00.270
And the locals brought them
to some collapsed grass

00:12:00.270 --> 00:12:03.930
houses made out of sod and wooden timber.

00:12:03.930 --> 00:12:07.090
And the locals thought
that they were the homes

00:12:07.090 --> 00:12:10.168
of native Canadians, first nations peoples

00:12:10.168 --> 00:12:14.393
but the Ingstads began to excavate there.

00:12:15.520 --> 00:12:20.520
And they found evidence of the Norse

00:12:20.920 --> 00:12:23.820
that I would say, I guess
I would say it was be

00:12:23.820 --> 00:12:25.810
persuasive to other archeologists

00:12:25.810 --> 00:12:29.763
but then they found this
bronze cloak fastening pin.

00:12:30.680 --> 00:12:35.680
And this was really a
convincing piece of evidence

00:12:37.340 --> 00:12:41.670
because it matched exactly
pins that had been found

00:12:41.670 --> 00:12:45.670
like that in Iceland and Denmark.

00:12:45.670 --> 00:12:48.230
And so we call it a diagnostic artifact.

00:12:48.230 --> 00:12:49.760
If you find such an artifact,

00:12:49.760 --> 00:12:52.660
it allows you to diagnose that you were

00:12:52.660 --> 00:12:56.903
in the presence of this X
society in this case, the Norse.

00:12:58.150 --> 00:13:01.640
And so once the Ingstads
did their excavations,

00:13:01.640 --> 00:13:04.130
there was no question that the Norse

00:13:04.130 --> 00:13:07.690
had arrived in Northeastern Canada

00:13:09.290 --> 00:13:12.240
but there was a question
about how far they went.

00:13:12.240 --> 00:13:15.710
Oh, I was gonna say, this
is one of their dwellings.

00:13:15.710 --> 00:13:17.490
It was the biggest dwelling.

00:13:17.490 --> 00:13:22.250
And this is where they found
other evidence of the Norse.

00:13:22.250 --> 00:13:25.284
They found some like a needle sharpener

00:13:25.284 --> 00:13:28.020
and a weight used in spinning.

00:13:28.020 --> 00:13:31.340
So with that tells us
that women were with them.

00:13:31.340 --> 00:13:34.000
And there was also a boat repair shed

00:13:34.000 --> 00:13:36.970
where there were fragments of iron nails.

00:13:36.970 --> 00:13:40.980
And the peoples of the
Americas were working metal

00:13:40.980 --> 00:13:43.740
around the year 1000, but
nobody was working iron.

00:13:43.740 --> 00:13:45.650
So we know that that was further

00:13:45.650 --> 00:13:48.853
evidence confirming the Norse presence.

00:13:50.405 --> 00:13:54.780
Then the question is, how
far south did the Vikings go

00:13:54.780 --> 00:13:59.620
from Newfoundland and
conventionally people think

00:13:59.620 --> 00:14:02.480
that they probably got to
somewhere around Maine.

00:14:02.480 --> 00:14:07.480
There's a Viking penny was
found in Maine and the evidence

00:14:08.807 --> 00:14:11.560
now I want to show you that
this is definitely speculative.

00:14:11.560 --> 00:14:15.640
This is not a hard clad case,

00:14:15.640 --> 00:14:20.150
but it's an interesting
possibility that they got

00:14:20.150 --> 00:14:25.020
as far as the Maya Capitol
here, big city, the biggest city

00:14:25.020 --> 00:14:28.309
in the America's probably
about 40,000 people live there.

00:14:28.309 --> 00:14:32.033
And it was thriving right
around the year 1000.

00:14:33.299 --> 00:14:36.740
And in Chichen Itza, which many of you

00:14:36.740 --> 00:14:38.380
have may have visited,

00:14:38.380 --> 00:14:41.750
this is a famous building
that people come and visit

00:14:41.750 --> 00:14:46.040
because on the equinox,
this pattern is formed

00:14:46.040 --> 00:14:49.690
of like the shadow of a dragon's body.

00:14:49.690 --> 00:14:54.690
And then the shadow ends
with the head of the dragon.

00:14:55.410 --> 00:14:58.130
So that tells you about the sophisticated

00:14:58.130 --> 00:15:01.244
astronomy that the Maya had,

00:15:01.244 --> 00:15:05.070
their command of astronomical knowledge.

00:15:05.070 --> 00:15:10.070
And at Chichen Itza was a
pool called the Sacred Cenote.

00:15:10.500 --> 00:15:12.960
And in the pool, the pool was used

00:15:12.960 --> 00:15:15.360
for various ritual offerings

00:15:15.360 --> 00:15:18.130
and archeologists have found things

00:15:18.130 --> 00:15:22.270
from other places that allow us to deduce

00:15:22.270 --> 00:15:25.530
that there was trade between
the Maya and other places.

00:15:25.530 --> 00:15:27.740
And I'll talk about a
couple of these examples,

00:15:27.740 --> 00:15:30.840
but basically if
archeologists find something

00:15:30.840 --> 00:15:33.730
that they know originates
from a distant place,

00:15:33.730 --> 00:15:35.630
they can posit that that was either

00:15:35.630 --> 00:15:37.780
came to the place where it was found

00:15:37.780 --> 00:15:40.460
either as a gift or a trade item.

00:15:40.460 --> 00:15:45.260
And turquoise is one of the
distinctive things that,

00:15:45.260 --> 00:15:49.060
distinctive items that
archeologists found in Chichen Itza.

00:15:49.060 --> 00:15:53.350
And the it's now possible to use this

00:15:53.350 --> 00:15:56.900
secondary ion mass
spectrometry to identify

00:15:56.900 --> 00:16:00.300
where turquoise has
been, an individual piece

00:16:00.300 --> 00:16:01.740
of turquoise has been mined.

00:16:01.740 --> 00:16:03.520
That analysis hasn't been done yet

00:16:03.520 --> 00:16:06.350
on the Mayan materials,
but this has been done

00:16:06.350 --> 00:16:10.230
on materials in Chaco
Canyon, in New Mexico.

00:16:10.230 --> 00:16:13.140
And it turns out that the
peoples of Chaco mined

00:16:13.140 --> 00:16:16.053
their turquoise in a
couple of different places,

00:16:16.920 --> 00:16:19.440
and in Chaco Canyon which many of you

00:16:19.440 --> 00:16:22.580
may also have been to,
there were some pots.

00:16:22.580 --> 00:16:27.020
These are very typical characteristic pots

00:16:27.020 --> 00:16:31.620
of the Chaco peoples and
the ancestral Puebloans.

00:16:31.620 --> 00:16:35.890
And on the bottom of these
pots were some residues.

00:16:35.890 --> 00:16:38.830
And then when they were
analyzed using this high

00:16:38.830 --> 00:16:40.636
performance liquid chromatography,

00:16:40.636 --> 00:16:43.330
the scientists doing the analysis,

00:16:43.330 --> 00:16:45.363
it was funded by Hershey Chocolate,

00:16:45.363 --> 00:16:48.610
found the traces of liquid chocolate.

00:16:48.610 --> 00:16:52.499
And so that allows us
to know that to conclude

00:16:52.499 --> 00:16:55.913
that there was a trade between the Maya.

00:16:56.910 --> 00:16:59.510
Their center is on the Yucatan peninsula.

00:16:59.510 --> 00:17:01.670
They're the only people
who are making chocolate

00:17:01.670 --> 00:17:03.460
in the Americas at this time,

00:17:03.460 --> 00:17:05.723
and the peoples living in Chaco.

00:17:07.480 --> 00:17:11.319
Also in the Sacred Cenote
where these gold objects

00:17:11.319 --> 00:17:14.200
and these have been located as coming

00:17:14.200 --> 00:17:17.780
from either Panama or Costa
Rica on the basis of style.

00:17:17.780 --> 00:17:21.240
Identical artifacts have been
found in those two countries.

00:17:21.240 --> 00:17:23.862
So that tells us that
the Maya were trading

00:17:23.862 --> 00:17:28.862
with peoples to the south, as
well as to their northwest.

00:17:30.932 --> 00:17:33.520
Let's go back to that
question about the Vikings

00:17:33.520 --> 00:17:37.400
and whether or not they got
to the Yucatan peninsula.

00:17:37.400 --> 00:17:40.540
This is a picture of The
Temple of the Warriors

00:17:40.540 --> 00:17:44.140
at Chichen Itza, and you are not,

00:17:44.140 --> 00:17:45.610
it's so many people go to the site

00:17:45.610 --> 00:17:48.150
now that it's no longer possible to go in

00:17:48.150 --> 00:17:52.550
but in 1920s and thirties there was a team

00:17:52.550 --> 00:17:55.670
from the Carnegie Institution that based

00:17:55.670 --> 00:17:58.810
in Washington that went
and recovered some murals.

00:17:58.810 --> 00:18:01.380
And most of the murals look like this.

00:18:01.380 --> 00:18:06.380
This is this scene of
invaders from one village.

00:18:06.400 --> 00:18:09.180
And the people have been painted in gray,

00:18:09.180 --> 00:18:13.640
the invaders, capturing the
people, the local people.

00:18:13.640 --> 00:18:16.470
And you can see that the local
people have stripes on them.

00:18:16.470 --> 00:18:21.470
So those may be tattooed warrior marks.

00:18:21.840 --> 00:18:25.340
They may just be a motif that
the artist use to distinguish

00:18:25.340 --> 00:18:27.290
between these two groups of people.

00:18:27.290 --> 00:18:30.410
I show you this because that's
a kind of typical picture

00:18:30.410 --> 00:18:34.580
and this is unfortunately
a watercolor copy

00:18:34.580 --> 00:18:37.450
of an original mural that
has now been destroyed.

00:18:37.450 --> 00:18:39.340
So all the evidence I'm going to talk

00:18:39.340 --> 00:18:42.170
about for the next couple
of slides is secondary.

00:18:42.170 --> 00:18:46.460
It's based, it's I think
fateful copies of materials

00:18:46.460 --> 00:18:49.680
that once existed in the
twenties and thirties,

00:18:49.680 --> 00:18:51.330
but then were destroyed after

00:18:51.330 --> 00:18:55.513
they had been copied by investigators.

00:18:56.670 --> 00:18:59.520
These are the pictures
that are quite intriguing

00:18:59.520 --> 00:19:01.800
because they show people.

00:19:01.800 --> 00:19:03.313
These are captives.

00:19:03.313 --> 00:19:04.980
I'll show you how I know that,

00:19:04.980 --> 00:19:06.180
but people in this slide,

00:19:06.180 --> 00:19:08.860
you can see that this
person has been thrown

00:19:08.860 --> 00:19:12.300
into the water and he's got blonde hair.

00:19:12.300 --> 00:19:14.100
He also has beads in his hair.

00:19:14.100 --> 00:19:16.710
And from other Mayan iconography

00:19:16.710 --> 00:19:20.710
we know that that's how
the Mayans portrayed POW's

00:19:22.150 --> 00:19:25.160
by putting and this he's floating there,

00:19:25.160 --> 00:19:27.700
and then this fish is looked menacingly

00:19:27.700 --> 00:19:31.670
as if it's about to bite his wrist.

00:19:31.670 --> 00:19:36.457
These murals, none of these
murals, the totality they're,

00:19:36.457 --> 00:19:40.850
I'm showing you details
because the they're so damaged.

00:19:40.850 --> 00:19:43.110
Here's another victim.

00:19:43.110 --> 00:19:47.564
Who's got those same beat
beads in his blonde hair.

00:19:47.564 --> 00:19:52.170
And then here's a victim
that we can see more clearly

00:19:52.170 --> 00:19:55.050
that he's got the blonde hair,

00:19:55.050 --> 00:19:56.802
and he also, his arms are bound

00:19:56.802 --> 00:20:01.360
behind him and he's been
thrown into the water.

00:20:01.360 --> 00:20:06.040
So the question is whether
these might have been Vikings

00:20:06.040 --> 00:20:10.940
and the, and if they were,
how did they get there?

00:20:10.940 --> 00:20:13.930
And what a big part of
trying to figure out

00:20:13.930 --> 00:20:15.150
who's moving around the world

00:20:15.150 --> 00:20:18.060
in the year 1000 and how they moved,

00:20:18.060 --> 00:20:19.920
has to do with these gyres,

00:20:19.920 --> 00:20:22.600
the currents that are in the ocean.

00:20:22.600 --> 00:20:24.660
And so this is a picture of all

00:20:24.660 --> 00:20:28.850
of the gyres in the world oceans.

00:20:28.850 --> 00:20:32.320
And then here's a detail of the gyre,

00:20:32.320 --> 00:20:34.450
the North Atlantic gyre.

00:20:34.450 --> 00:20:38.350
And you can see, that
if and the red gyre's

00:20:38.350 --> 00:20:40.639
on the surface of the
water, the blue gyre,

00:20:40.639 --> 00:20:45.639
the blue line is the deep
water under the Atlantic.

00:20:46.620 --> 00:20:50.600
And so if someone, for example
had been blown off course,

00:20:50.600 --> 00:20:53.230
traveling from Greenland to Newfoundland

00:20:53.230 --> 00:20:57.510
and the sagas report that
from multiple ships were blown

00:20:57.510 --> 00:21:02.187
off course and had
connected with the gyre,

00:21:02.187 --> 00:21:05.950
the gyre could have
brought them ultimately

00:21:05.950 --> 00:21:08.810
to the Yucatan peninsula.

00:21:08.810 --> 00:21:11.860
And I warned you that
this is speculative right?

00:21:11.860 --> 00:21:15.850
We have no hard evidence that they arrived

00:21:15.850 --> 00:21:19.440
but it's possible the ocean
currents would have facilitated

00:21:19.440 --> 00:21:23.660
or would, it would have
been possible to be blown

00:21:23.660 --> 00:21:26.970
up on shore to the Yucatan peninsula.

00:21:26.970 --> 00:21:28.510
And there's one more piece of evidence

00:21:28.510 --> 00:21:32.090
which is I think, well,
I was gonna say my,

00:21:32.090 --> 00:21:35.690
our late colleague Mike
Coe, the great Maya expert,

00:21:35.690 --> 00:21:38.160
he thought this evidence
was more compelling

00:21:38.160 --> 00:21:41.260
than the murals of the POW's

00:21:41.260 --> 00:21:45.690
that this boat you can
see is made with planks.

00:21:45.690 --> 00:21:48.590
And they, you can see the
outline of the planks here.

00:21:48.590 --> 00:21:51.710
There's, nobody's shown in this picture.

00:21:51.710 --> 00:21:54.280
This is from a different
building at Chichen Itza,

00:21:54.280 --> 00:21:58.290
who is a Viking or a possible Viking

00:21:58.290 --> 00:22:03.290
but so, he, Mike Coe, proposed
that this boat was captured

00:22:04.510 --> 00:22:08.173
from the Vikings and
then the Maya used it.

00:22:10.650 --> 00:22:13.500
And I'll just give you a comparison

00:22:13.500 --> 00:22:17.550
so that you can see a Viking ship.

00:22:17.550 --> 00:22:18.860
These are preserved.

00:22:18.860 --> 00:22:20.813
This is in a museum in Norway.

00:22:20.813 --> 00:22:22.550
And these have been these were excavated

00:22:22.550 --> 00:22:26.593
in the late 1800's and have
been preserved remarkably well.

00:22:27.720 --> 00:22:32.420
And there's also a legend that

00:22:32.420 --> 00:22:35.680
a Spanish explorer heard in 1588.

00:22:35.680 --> 00:22:38.500
And, you know, we may
or may not believe this

00:22:38.500 --> 00:22:41.540
but when he was going on along

00:22:41.540 --> 00:22:43.620
the coast of the Yucatan peninsula,

00:22:43.620 --> 00:22:48.240
he met local peoples who
told him about 70 Moros.

00:22:48.240 --> 00:22:50.877
So he's writing in Spanish

00:22:50.877 --> 00:22:53.650
and this meaning black people,

00:22:53.650 --> 00:22:56.690
I mean presumably from Africa.

00:22:56.690 --> 00:22:59.550
They called their leader Xeque

00:22:59.550 --> 00:23:03.830
which could be derived from sheik.

00:23:03.830 --> 00:23:05.840
And then chakan is the local word

00:23:05.840 --> 00:23:08.310
for Savannah that doesn't
have anyone living on it.

00:23:08.310 --> 00:23:11.720
So I mentioned that again
just as the possibility

00:23:11.720 --> 00:23:15.050
that people could be washed up on shore

00:23:15.050 --> 00:23:19.520
and land on the Yucatan peninsula.

