of a careful examination of
a lot of carbon-14 samples

00:31:12.020 --> 00:31:16.660
and consciously looking at

00:31:16.660 --> 00:31:20.170
smaller like plant lives
like leaves and twigs

00:31:20.170 --> 00:31:21.510
as opposed to charcoal

00:31:21.510 --> 00:31:24.650
because charcoal can live for a long time.

00:31:24.650 --> 00:31:27.950
And the contention of Janet Wilmshurst

00:31:27.950 --> 00:31:30.030
and her collaborators was that

00:31:31.450 --> 00:31:33.660
the charcoal dates were misleading

00:31:33.660 --> 00:31:38.660
'cause they suggested a the settlement

00:31:38.900 --> 00:31:40.970
of the islands several hundred years

00:31:40.970 --> 00:31:44.333
before the dates that she proposes.

00:31:47.060 --> 00:31:52.060
And some of the information
we have about the settlement

00:31:52.900 --> 00:31:55.820
of the Pacific is very
late from when James Cook,

00:31:55.820 --> 00:31:59.060
and actually there's some
Dutch Voyagers who traveled

00:31:59.060 --> 00:32:03.573
in the 1600's and then
James Cook in the 1770s.

00:32:04.890 --> 00:32:09.890
And these paintings and drawings,
sketches, give us a sense

00:32:10.100 --> 00:32:14.046
of the vessels that the
Polynesians were using at the time.

00:32:14.046 --> 00:32:17.490
Here are the voyagers are wearing masks.

00:32:17.490 --> 00:32:19.070
The rowers are wearing masks.

00:32:19.070 --> 00:32:22.040
This is when they are
coming out to meet Cook.

00:32:22.040 --> 00:32:24.483
And this is some kind of a ritual.

00:32:25.370 --> 00:32:28.054
That's why they're holding this image here

00:32:28.054 --> 00:32:33.054
but the pictures show two canoes
connected and then a sale.

00:32:36.310 --> 00:32:37.340
And the...

00:32:39.304 --> 00:32:43.680
We know from this information
from the 1700's to 1800's

00:32:43.680 --> 00:32:48.680
that the Polynesians could
load a lot of possessions

00:32:48.977 --> 00:32:51.780
and including animals onto the framework

00:32:51.780 --> 00:32:54.770
between the two canoes
as they were crossing

00:32:54.770 --> 00:32:56.260
from one Island to another.

00:32:56.260 --> 00:32:58.200
We don't know what kind of vessel

00:32:58.200 --> 00:33:00.920
they were using in the year 1000.

00:33:00.920 --> 00:33:02.850
I think many people think
that they were using

00:33:02.850 --> 00:33:04.730
this kind of vessel, but we don't know

00:33:04.730 --> 00:33:06.810
that there hasn't been any
archaeological evidence

00:33:06.810 --> 00:33:09.203
of the kind of vessels that they used.

00:33:11.070 --> 00:33:14.860
And then some of the information

00:33:14.860 --> 00:33:17.670
we have about how they did this comes

00:33:17.670 --> 00:33:22.670
from the late 20th century
from indigenous informants.

00:33:23.250 --> 00:33:26.270
This is a man named Mau Piailug who

00:33:26.270 --> 00:33:31.270
had studied traditional
Polynesian seafaring as a child.

00:33:31.480 --> 00:33:34.293
And here he's teaching it to his children.

00:33:35.200 --> 00:33:38.770
And this allowed the Polynesians,

00:33:38.770 --> 00:33:42.350
they basically they did
all their navigation

00:33:42.350 --> 00:33:44.250
without any navigational instruments.

00:33:44.250 --> 00:33:46.100
This is also true of the Vikings.

00:33:46.100 --> 00:33:48.810
The Vikings are going shorter distances

00:33:48.810 --> 00:33:50.890
and they can see in many cases,

00:33:50.890 --> 00:33:53.163
they can see where they're going.

00:33:53.163 --> 00:33:57.970
But the Polynesians are
orient themselves by looking

00:33:57.970 --> 00:34:01.800
at the sky, looking at the
configuration of the stars.

00:34:01.800 --> 00:34:04.280
Of course, it varies by the time of year.

00:34:04.280 --> 00:34:07.990
It also varies by your
position in the Pacific.

00:34:07.990 --> 00:34:11.830
And so they can memorize
these various sets of stars.

00:34:11.830 --> 00:34:13.700
That's what Mao is teaching here

00:34:13.700 --> 00:34:16.270
and that allowed them to orient
themselves when they were

00:34:16.270 --> 00:34:20.950
in the ocean and they were
not near any landmasses.

00:34:20.950 --> 00:34:24.950
And the illustration of this,

00:34:24.950 --> 00:34:28.320
the power of this system, of
this traditional system was

00:34:28.320 --> 00:34:33.320
that Mau was able to navigate
all the way from Hawaii

00:34:33.710 --> 00:34:38.710
to Tahiti and his home, he's
from the area around Guam.

00:34:42.440 --> 00:34:47.440
So he's far from home, but in this voyage,

00:34:47.730 --> 00:34:50.330
he does this voyage for the first time.

00:34:50.330 --> 00:34:52.610
And he navigates a craft all the way

00:34:52.610 --> 00:34:55.340
from Hawaii to Tahiti using

00:34:56.300 --> 00:35:01.300
this set of knowledge that
he's learned from his elders.

00:35:02.250 --> 00:35:05.090
And so that I think is again,

00:35:05.090 --> 00:35:09.570
we don't know for sure how
the Polynesians navigated

00:35:09.570 --> 00:35:12.620
in this period between 1200 and 1290,

00:35:12.620 --> 00:35:15.930
but it's very suggestive that
there's a traditional form

00:35:15.930 --> 00:35:18.623
of navigation that allows them,

00:35:18.623 --> 00:35:21.250
that allowed them to cover this kind

00:35:21.250 --> 00:35:25.130
of distances in the late 20th century.

00:35:25.130 --> 00:35:29.590
It's very plausible that
would have used it earlier.

00:35:29.590 --> 00:35:31.190
I was going to say, there's
a funny story about Mau

00:35:31.190 --> 00:35:36.190
that he near the end of his
life, he is blown off course,

00:35:36.240 --> 00:35:38.560
and he's two weeks late coming home.

00:35:38.560 --> 00:35:40.110
And his family calls the coast guard

00:35:40.110 --> 00:35:41.490
'cause they're concerned about him.

00:35:41.490 --> 00:35:43.770
Coast guard catches up with him and says,

00:35:43.770 --> 00:35:45.160
basically we can we take you home?

00:35:45.160 --> 00:35:46.040
And he said, no, no no.

00:35:46.040 --> 00:35:47.670
I know exactly where I am.

00:35:47.670 --> 00:35:48.960
I've been in this storm.

00:35:48.960 --> 00:35:52.180
I've been blown off
course, but leave me alone.

00:35:52.180 --> 00:35:53.013
I'm going to get home.

00:35:53.013 --> 00:35:55.570
And he does, he's he shakes them off.

00:35:55.570 --> 00:36:00.570
So the DNA report, this
was from the summer

00:36:02.120 --> 00:36:04.340
and this was in Nature,

00:36:04.340 --> 00:36:06.470
and there's some controversy about it.

00:36:06.470 --> 00:36:07.580
This is where I hope maybe somebody

00:36:07.580 --> 00:36:09.233
in the audience can help me.

00:36:10.950 --> 00:36:15.200
The contribution of the report is that

00:36:15.200 --> 00:36:18.380
the investigators looked
not just at Easter Island.

00:36:18.380 --> 00:36:20.610
People have always thought
Easter Island would be the place

00:36:20.610 --> 00:36:22.671
where there would be the most contact

00:36:22.671 --> 00:36:27.110
between the the likeliest
site of contact between

00:36:27.110 --> 00:36:32.110
the Polynesian islands and the
west coast of South America.

00:36:32.330 --> 00:36:36.790
But these investigators
looked also at DNA samples

00:36:36.790 --> 00:36:39.970
from farther north, from
the Marquesas Islands.

00:36:39.970 --> 00:36:42.160
And that's where if you were riding

00:36:42.160 --> 00:36:45.610
the Humboldt Current along the west coast,

00:36:45.610 --> 00:36:49.313
going north along the west
coast of Latin America,

00:36:50.330 --> 00:36:52.770
you would arrive

00:36:54.160 --> 00:36:56.560
north, far north of Easter Island,

00:36:56.560 --> 00:36:58.690
much closer to the equator.

00:36:58.690 --> 00:37:03.690
So they have a couple of
individuals whose DNA shows

00:37:05.450 --> 00:37:10.400
they say a specific moment of
contact, probably around 1200

00:37:10.400 --> 00:37:13.871
which matches nicely with
that archaeological study

00:37:13.871 --> 00:37:16.890
I told you about the carbon-14 dates

00:37:16.890 --> 00:37:19.180
from the different plants.

00:37:19.180 --> 00:37:21.380
And so they propose that.

00:37:21.380 --> 00:37:24.740
And then, but this, I
think it would be, I think

00:37:24.740 --> 00:37:28.231
more convincing if they
had ancient skeletons

00:37:28.231 --> 00:37:32.310
or pre-1492 skeletons,

00:37:32.310 --> 00:37:37.310
that pre-1492 DNA that allowed
them, that showed this.

00:37:38.050 --> 00:37:43.050
So far, my understanding is
that no pre-1492 DNA samples

00:37:44.500 --> 00:37:49.080
have surfaced that show the
mixing of the Native Americans

00:37:49.080 --> 00:37:54.080
with, or the Native Latin
Americans with the Polynesians.

00:37:54.140 --> 00:37:59.140
And there is some criticism
from two other investigators

00:38:01.750 --> 00:38:03.160
who are working on this kind of

00:38:03.160 --> 00:38:05.200
movement of ancient populations.

00:38:05.200 --> 00:38:08.690
And they're very skeptical
about the results.

00:38:08.690 --> 00:38:12.400
A part of this has to
do with who was moving

00:38:12.400 --> 00:38:14.680
and whether the people who were

00:38:14.680 --> 00:38:17.990
in Latin America had a seafaring tradition

00:38:17.990 --> 00:38:20.670
or a boat building tradition
that would have allowed them

00:38:20.670 --> 00:38:24.250
to go into the Pacific, or
whether it's more likely

00:38:24.250 --> 00:38:26.900
that the Polynesians traveled all the way

00:38:26.900 --> 00:38:29.720
from the Marquesas or Easter Island

00:38:29.720 --> 00:38:32.150
to Latin America and back.

00:38:32.150 --> 00:38:35.260
And this also raises the ghost of

00:38:36.778 --> 00:38:41.778
Thor Heyerdahl who was convinced
that people had traveled

00:38:42.350 --> 00:38:47.303
from Latin America to
the Polynesian Islands.

00:38:48.540 --> 00:38:53.540
But I was gonna say very few
people were convinced by his

00:38:54.364 --> 00:38:57.820
I mean, I think everybody
knew about Kon-Tiki

00:38:57.820 --> 00:39:01.430
and his voyages, but very
few scholars were convinced

00:39:01.430 --> 00:39:04.237
that the voyage was possible.

00:39:04.237 --> 00:39:06.750
One of the objections is that when

00:39:06.750 --> 00:39:10.243
he did this reconstruction of the voyage,

00:39:11.400 --> 00:39:15.420
his raft was towed into off the coast

00:39:15.420 --> 00:39:18.373
for about 50 miles
before he began the trip.

00:39:20.400 --> 00:39:22.650
And so that's I was gonna say

00:39:22.650 --> 00:39:25.430
the exploration of the Pacific.

00:39:25.430 --> 00:39:30.430
There's another set of voyages
by these are the speakers.

00:39:30.980 --> 00:39:34.680
So the Malayan language
is related to Polynesian

00:39:34.680 --> 00:39:39.680
and the modern population
of Madagascar speaks

00:39:39.730 --> 00:39:43.210
the Malagasy language,
which is related to Malay.

00:39:43.210 --> 00:39:46.280
So there's linguistic
evidence of a connection

00:39:46.280 --> 00:39:50.350
between the Malay
peninsula and Madagascar.

00:39:50.350 --> 00:39:55.070
There's also cultivation of
rice and Southeast Asian crops.

00:39:55.070 --> 00:39:58.540
And that and then here,

00:39:58.540 --> 00:40:01.497
you can see this other set of voyages.

00:40:01.497 --> 00:40:04.360
These took place actually
before the year 1000.

00:40:04.360 --> 00:40:09.360
They start around 500 and they
go across the Indian ocean.

00:40:10.540 --> 00:40:14.180
And these have been demonstrated

00:40:14.180 --> 00:40:19.180
by careful analysis of
the plants in Madagascar

00:40:20.280 --> 00:40:23.250
versus the plants that are
on the African mainland.

00:40:23.250 --> 00:40:28.090
And the plants on Madagascar
have produced many more,

00:40:28.090 --> 00:40:30.410
much more evidence of a rice diet.

00:40:30.410 --> 00:40:33.850
The only places on the African mainland

00:40:33.850 --> 00:40:36.410
that have comparable finds of rice

00:40:36.410 --> 00:40:39.370
are sea coast or sea ports.

00:40:39.370 --> 00:40:42.440
And those are presumably where people

00:40:42.440 --> 00:40:45.170
from Asia merchants from Asia lived.

00:40:45.170 --> 00:40:50.170
So the, I was gonna say,
this is a case where really

00:40:51.230 --> 00:40:54.260
all of the evidence is
either archeological

00:40:54.260 --> 00:40:58.513
or ethnohistorical is
transmitted from, you know

00:40:59.860 --> 00:41:02.650
was used, either the
information from James Cook

00:41:02.650 --> 00:41:07.340
or the information from the
late 20th century voyagers

00:41:07.340 --> 00:41:08.990
or the anthropologist who studied

00:41:08.990 --> 00:41:13.000
with them to reconstruct the mechanism

00:41:13.000 --> 00:41:18.000
of the navigation system, the workings.

