that the reason the
Chinese are going there is

00:47:21.750 --> 00:47:25.520
that they want to import these different

00:47:25.520 --> 00:47:28.220
aromatics from Southeast Asian.

00:47:28.220 --> 00:47:30.050
Aromatic is catch word use.

00:47:30.050 --> 00:47:34.780
The Chinese word is xiang,
meaning fragrant items.

00:47:34.780 --> 00:47:38.740
And these include things like sandalwood.

00:47:38.740 --> 00:47:42.603
They include spices, like cloves.

00:47:43.780 --> 00:47:45.960
They're some natural products.

00:47:45.960 --> 00:47:48.320
These aren't necessarily aromatics

00:47:48.320 --> 00:47:51.680
but like the, the feathers
of the King Fisher bird

00:47:53.090 --> 00:47:55.860
were, the Chinese valued very much.

00:47:55.860 --> 00:47:59.080
And when I tell you about
these things, you can think,

00:47:59.080 --> 00:48:00.830
oh well, the Chinese were importing those.

00:48:00.830 --> 00:48:02.420
They couldn't have been that important

00:48:02.420 --> 00:48:06.760
but the society in China, and we know this

00:48:06.760 --> 00:48:10.690
from around the year 1000,
and then the use of explodes

00:48:10.690 --> 00:48:12.630
in the next couple of centuries.

00:48:12.630 --> 00:48:14.710
People are consumed with trying to change

00:48:14.710 --> 00:48:16.370
the way that they smell

00:48:16.370 --> 00:48:18.855
and the way that the air
in their houses smell.

00:48:18.855 --> 00:48:21.950
And they burn a lot of
different kinds of instance

00:48:21.950 --> 00:48:26.430
and fragrant woods to try
to change their environment.

00:48:26.430 --> 00:48:29.710
And the, so those goods are,

00:48:29.710 --> 00:48:32.560
the Chinese are importing
in large quantities

00:48:32.560 --> 00:48:35.560
and mostly from Southeast Asia,
but some of them also come

00:48:35.560 --> 00:48:39.463
from India and come from
the Arabian peninsula.

00:48:42.360 --> 00:48:46.930
What the Chinese are selling,
they've got ceramics.

00:48:46.930 --> 00:48:50.580
This is a classic example,
and so this is a slide

00:48:50.580 --> 00:48:53.520
from my teaching about the
iPhone of the year, 1000.

00:48:53.520 --> 00:48:55.867
The thing the item that
everybody had to have

00:48:55.867 --> 00:49:00.867
was a high fired, translucent ceramic pot.

00:49:02.100 --> 00:49:03.860
They were very easy to clean

00:49:03.860 --> 00:49:06.610
and much easier to clean than pots made

00:49:06.610 --> 00:49:10.570
in other parts of the world
that were not fired as high.

00:49:10.570 --> 00:49:13.760
The Chinese are also
exporting metals, ingots,

00:49:13.760 --> 00:49:16.379
and so ingots of copper and tin.

00:49:16.379 --> 00:49:20.480
They're making metal objects
like cauldrons and woks

00:49:20.480 --> 00:49:23.170
and mirrors, also some
weapons, but we actually don't,

00:49:23.170 --> 00:49:25.670
the Chinese are using a lot
of those weapons within China.

00:49:25.670 --> 00:49:28.460
We don't have much evidence
of their exporting them,

00:49:28.460 --> 00:49:31.590
and high quality fabrics,
both cottons and silks.

00:49:31.590 --> 00:49:34.400
And we have much more
archaeological evidence about

00:49:34.400 --> 00:49:39.400
the ceramics than we do about
the metals or the textiles.

00:49:39.580 --> 00:49:42.480
And the main thing that
the archaeology tells us

00:49:42.480 --> 00:49:44.680
that the written records
don't is the quantity.

00:49:44.680 --> 00:49:46.610
So we have descriptions of ships

00:49:46.610 --> 00:49:49.435
leaving China that are carrying ceramics

00:49:49.435 --> 00:49:52.300
but we have archaeological sites.

00:49:52.300 --> 00:49:55.470
One, ship found in Indonesian waters,

00:49:55.470 --> 00:49:57.400
one shipwreck found in Indonesian waters

00:49:57.400 --> 00:50:02.400
has 600,000 pots were found onboard.

00:50:03.130 --> 00:50:06.080
It's just a quantity
that the written sources

00:50:06.080 --> 00:50:07.803
just give no hint of.

00:50:09.470 --> 00:50:13.720
And this, we also have some shipwrecks

00:50:13.720 --> 00:50:16.300
the actual ships that survived.

00:50:16.300 --> 00:50:18.860
This one is in a Chinese museum.

00:50:18.860 --> 00:50:23.100
And it's interesting
because it has repair.

00:50:23.100 --> 00:50:25.020
So you can see the Chinese made this

00:50:25.020 --> 00:50:27.220
with planking like the Vikings did, right?

00:50:27.220 --> 00:50:28.053
They're different kinds.

00:50:28.053 --> 00:50:28.886
It's a different construction,

00:50:28.886 --> 00:50:31.710
but there's and then the Chinese ships

00:50:31.710 --> 00:50:33.480
had distinct compartments.

00:50:33.480 --> 00:50:36.140
My next slide will make that clearer.

00:50:36.140 --> 00:50:37.090
No, it won't, sorry.

00:50:37.090 --> 00:50:39.310
I cut that slide because of time.

00:50:39.310 --> 00:50:42.270
The Chinese ships had
distinct compartments,

00:50:42.270 --> 00:50:45.140
separate compartments,
and those meant that,

00:50:45.140 --> 00:50:47.460
they could, that question
we had before about water,

00:50:47.460 --> 00:50:49.700
they could travel with a compartment that

00:50:49.700 --> 00:50:52.576
was filled with fresh water
and they also sometimes

00:50:52.576 --> 00:50:55.090
traveled with a compartment
filled with seawater,

00:50:55.090 --> 00:50:57.910
where they had fish living in the water

00:50:57.910 --> 00:50:59.320
they could kill when they wanted to.

00:50:59.320 --> 00:51:04.320
But the real advantage of
those discreet compartments was

00:51:04.700 --> 00:51:09.700
that if the ship sprang a
leak, the leak was contained

00:51:10.180 --> 00:51:12.250
in that one area and not in the whole,

00:51:12.250 --> 00:51:14.910
it didn't affect the whole ship.

00:51:14.910 --> 00:51:18.110
In my talk title, I mentioned LIDAR

00:51:18.110 --> 00:51:22.640
and the contribution of
LIDAR to our understanding

00:51:22.640 --> 00:51:25.840
really of Southeast Asia is that,

00:51:25.840 --> 00:51:28.773
and you can see here that
this is the same place.

00:51:29.670 --> 00:51:32.740
This Ta Prohm Temple in Angkor Wat,

00:51:32.740 --> 00:51:37.740
and when you visit it, you
see all these trees around it.

00:51:38.020 --> 00:51:42.490
And it would be, it was very difficult

00:51:42.490 --> 00:51:46.270
for archeologists to know whether

00:51:46.270 --> 00:51:48.930
basically what the terrain looked like,

00:51:48.930 --> 00:51:51.990
what the surface looked like
'cause there was so much

00:51:51.990 --> 00:51:54.887
undergrowth in this tropical forest,

00:51:54.887 --> 00:51:58.930
and here is a LIDAR picture that,

00:51:58.930 --> 00:52:02.520
so this is taken by bombarding the ground

00:52:02.520 --> 00:52:06.410
with rays and then editing
them, filtering them out.

00:52:06.410 --> 00:52:08.770
And so anything that bounces back

00:52:08.770 --> 00:52:10.510
from a plant is filtered out.

00:52:10.510 --> 00:52:14.140
And so that archeologists can
see the surface of the land.

00:52:14.140 --> 00:52:18.130
And the real surprise from
Angkor Wat was how many

00:52:18.130 --> 00:52:20.598
of these, what are now open spaces

00:52:20.598 --> 00:52:23.570
or a forest had spaces historically

00:52:23.570 --> 00:52:25.820
contained many, many dwellings.

00:52:25.820 --> 00:52:30.477
And the upshot is that
Angkor Wat's population,

00:52:30.477 --> 00:52:34.410
the estimate has been increased
to about 750,000 people

00:52:34.410 --> 00:52:37.480
which is far higher than
what was previously thought.

00:52:37.480 --> 00:52:42.480
And there's nothing, there's
no information like that in any

00:52:42.550 --> 00:52:47.213
of the written Chinese sources
about Cambodia at this time.

00:52:51.920 --> 00:52:55.250
And then the last slide

00:52:55.250 --> 00:52:58.340
about the Chinese is
showing you this route

00:52:58.340 --> 00:53:02.320
that they traveled and the
distances that they covered.

00:53:02.320 --> 00:53:05.520
I mean in some ways the I'm going to apply

00:53:05.520 --> 00:53:07.313
that the farther people went,

00:53:07.313 --> 00:53:09.550
I mean the more impressive it was

00:53:09.550 --> 00:53:14.550
but in fact, cutting directly
across here saved quite a lot

00:53:15.720 --> 00:53:20.720
of miles and that this route
all the way from Guangzhou

00:53:20.940 --> 00:53:23.920
to Basra and then down to, Sofala,

00:53:23.920 --> 00:53:27.490
south to Sofala, this is about three times

00:53:27.490 --> 00:53:30.883
the length of Columbus's voyages.

00:53:32.690 --> 00:53:33.523
So...

00:53:35.308 --> 00:53:37.530
I was gonna say just in
terms of the distances

00:53:37.530 --> 00:53:41.080
that were traveled, some of
the voyages that take place

00:53:41.080 --> 00:53:42.390
in what we conventionally call the age

00:53:42.390 --> 00:53:46.420
of exploration were not that much longer.

00:53:46.420 --> 00:53:49.653
Columbus's voyages were on a par

00:53:49.653 --> 00:53:52.710
with those of the Malayo-Polynesians,

00:53:52.710 --> 00:53:54.610
and actually shorter than those that

00:53:54.610 --> 00:53:57.660
the Chinese were traveling
as they went from.

00:53:57.660 --> 00:54:00.100
And when I say the Chinese,
other peoples, Indian,

00:54:00.100 --> 00:54:02.770
other peoples in the Indian
ocean are also voyaging

00:54:02.770 --> 00:54:06.653
from the Islamic world
and India towards China.

00:54:08.642 --> 00:54:12.406
And so Da Gama's voyage where
he circumnavigates Africa,

00:54:12.406 --> 00:54:15.670
even he is not going
farther than the Chinese.

00:54:15.670 --> 00:54:16.530
Magellan, yes.

00:54:16.530 --> 00:54:19.530
Magellan goes around with his
crew, he gets killed right,

00:54:19.530 --> 00:54:21.250
in a rebellion in the Philippines,

00:54:21.250 --> 00:54:24.940
but Magellan circumnavigates the world.

00:54:24.940 --> 00:54:27.520
And so, but I was going to say

00:54:27.520 --> 00:54:29.860
the reason that we pay so much attention

00:54:29.860 --> 00:54:34.840
to the later voyages is that
we know about their impact,

00:54:34.840 --> 00:54:37.190
that they had any nearly immediate

00:54:37.190 --> 00:54:39.261
and far reaching consequences.

00:54:39.261 --> 00:54:43.890
There were many of the
people living in the Americas

00:54:43.890 --> 00:54:48.890
were killed by either the
common cold or flu or smallpox.

00:54:49.430 --> 00:54:53.730
And once America had been
emptied of the indigenous

00:54:53.730 --> 00:54:57.070
of many of the indigenous
peoples, then European settlers

00:54:57.070 --> 00:55:00.490
and African slaves
moved into the new world

00:55:00.490 --> 00:55:02.870
and there was the creation
of the triangle trade.

00:55:02.870 --> 00:55:05.860
So I mean, that's a huge impact.

00:55:05.860 --> 00:55:08.750
And the voyage is I'm talking
about in the year 1000

00:55:08.750 --> 00:55:13.130
or around the year 1000, the
impact is not as immediate.

00:55:13.130 --> 00:55:18.130
It's not as obvious but
they did affect people

00:55:18.510 --> 00:55:21.190
who were living in different regions.

00:55:21.190 --> 00:55:24.220
And so the Vikings don't have
much impact on North America.

00:55:24.220 --> 00:55:26.150
They pull out after about 10 years,

00:55:26.150 --> 00:55:30.791
but they traveled east into
Russia and the Ukraine.

00:55:30.791 --> 00:55:33.100
This was discussed in that article

00:55:33.100 --> 00:55:36.400
in the New York Times
yesterday about the DNA.

00:55:36.400 --> 00:55:40.800
And they built the first empires

00:55:40.800 --> 00:55:44.540
in the Russ empire is

00:55:44.540 --> 00:55:47.040
the product of Vikings who have

00:55:47.040 --> 00:55:49.680
or Swedes who have come, mostly Swedes,

00:55:49.680 --> 00:55:52.100
people living in modern
Sweden who have come

00:55:52.100 --> 00:55:54.190
into modern day Russia and Ukraine

00:55:54.190 --> 00:55:57.110
and create the Russ state.

00:55:57.110 --> 00:56:01.170
All of the Pacific and
Madagascar gets settled

00:56:01.170 --> 00:56:02.790
by the Polynesian voyages,

00:56:02.790 --> 00:56:04.800
so that's very clear impact.

00:56:04.800 --> 00:56:09.340
And the Chinese economy becomes
very heavily intertwined

00:56:09.340 --> 00:56:11.330
with that of Southeast Asia

00:56:11.330 --> 00:56:15.720
so much so that we have people who had

00:56:15.720 --> 00:56:17.140
been working the land in China

00:56:17.140 --> 00:56:20.850
who shift to working full
time in ceramics factories.

00:56:20.850 --> 00:56:24.110
And we have forest peoples
in Southeast Asia who

00:56:25.180 --> 00:56:27.810
you know, traditionally
were hunting and gathering

00:56:27.810 --> 00:56:32.074
and they start to hunt
and gather full time

00:56:32.074 --> 00:56:35.430
for specific animals like
those Kingfisher birds

00:56:35.430 --> 00:56:37.683
I told you about the Chinese want.

00:56:38.860 --> 00:56:43.330
So and all of this economic activity from,

00:56:43.330 --> 00:56:46.210
and these connections that
are formed in the year 1000,

00:56:46.210 --> 00:56:51.210
contribute to the connecting
up of the world in,

00:56:51.890 --> 00:56:54.470
the further connecting
up of the world in 1500.

