to Madagascar would've come

01:02:18.770 --> 01:02:21.420
from the Malay peninsula
and not from Africa

01:02:21.420 --> 01:02:22.820
but that is indeed the case.

01:02:22.820 --> 01:02:25.784
And we know that archaeologically
because of this evidence

01:02:25.784 --> 01:02:29.307
of the many, many different plants

01:02:29.307 --> 01:02:32.990
and many multiple sites
where they found the evidence

01:02:32.990 --> 01:02:36.190
of the rice and other
Asian crops on Madagascar

01:02:36.190 --> 01:02:39.140
and then a different set of more

01:02:39.140 --> 01:02:41.373
typical African crops on Africa.

01:02:42.450 --> 01:02:44.630
- From one of our new Franke Fellows.

01:02:44.630 --> 01:02:46.560
When you set out to write this book,

01:02:46.560 --> 01:02:48.550
did you know that, did you know then that

01:02:48.550 --> 01:02:51.840
you'd need to expand your
toolkit beyond texts?

01:02:51.840 --> 01:02:54.510
And how did your method
develop through the course

01:02:54.510 --> 01:02:57.270
of your research to
engage different methods

01:02:57.270 --> 01:02:59.403
such as archaeology, LIDAR, et cetera?

01:03:01.650 --> 01:03:03.100
- Thank you for the question.

01:03:05.770 --> 01:03:07.960
Two of the maps I showed
are from a world history

01:03:07.960 --> 01:03:10.470
textbook that I wrote co-wrote with,

01:03:10.470 --> 01:03:12.130
my coauthor's named Ken Curtis.

01:03:12.130 --> 01:03:14.700
And when he and I started
to write that book

01:03:14.700 --> 01:03:19.180
I didn't realize how important
these other different,

01:03:19.180 --> 01:03:21.874
these other methods, these
scientific methods would be.

01:03:21.874 --> 01:03:24.390
And then as we were working on that book

01:03:24.390 --> 01:03:28.560
and it took a long time,
I've realized that, you know,

01:03:28.560 --> 01:03:31.450
it was because for the mandate
of a world history textbook

01:03:31.450 --> 01:03:35.220
is each part of the world
gets its own chapter, right?

01:03:35.220 --> 01:03:38.220
You're not allowed to say,
oh, I can't write anything

01:03:38.220 --> 01:03:40.680
about Southeast Asia 'cause
we don't know anything.

01:03:40.680 --> 01:03:42.480
You have to find out what we know.

01:03:42.480 --> 01:03:46.080
So that's when I started
to expand my toolkit

01:03:46.080 --> 01:03:49.230
beyond texts, and I was always
interested in archaeology.

01:03:49.230 --> 01:03:51.270
I, in high school, I was
interested in archaeology

01:03:51.270 --> 01:03:53.890
and actually my earlier research

01:03:53.890 --> 01:03:57.130
I'm interested in excavated texts.

01:03:57.130 --> 01:04:00.860
So texts, that in the ground,
that archeologists find.

01:04:00.860 --> 01:04:03.230
We have such a long
transmitted historical record

01:04:03.230 --> 01:04:07.607
from China, but we have
very few texts that survive

01:04:08.630 --> 01:04:10.150
in the condition that they were written

01:04:10.150 --> 01:04:12.080
in, and then we can look at them today.

01:04:12.080 --> 01:04:14.170
And those are the things that I still find

01:04:14.170 --> 01:04:15.483
very compelling.

01:04:16.730 --> 01:04:18.480
- And follow--
(Valerie mumbling)

01:04:18.480 --> 01:04:19.470
- I was just going to say one more thing

01:04:19.470 --> 01:04:22.470
about the research method is, you know

01:04:22.470 --> 01:04:25.300
Yale is a great place in so many ways,

01:04:25.300 --> 01:04:29.210
but I've just found out
if I wanted to find out

01:04:29.210 --> 01:04:31.900
about different kinds of technologies

01:04:31.900 --> 01:04:35.250
or science technologies
that I don't know about,

01:04:35.250 --> 01:04:36.540
that I could go talk to colleagues

01:04:36.540 --> 01:04:39.820
and they would very generously
tell me what to read

01:04:39.820 --> 01:04:42.820
or explain things to me
when I read what they,

01:04:42.820 --> 01:04:45.610
when I did my reading and
I still didn't understand,

01:04:45.610 --> 01:04:47.360
they would patiently explain to me.

01:04:48.440 --> 01:04:50.207
- Have you followed the early Dutch

01:04:50.207 --> 01:04:54.040
cartographic history of South Asia?

01:04:54.040 --> 01:04:57.810
- No, but I think it'll
be interesting, you know,

01:04:57.810 --> 01:05:02.240
but it's I was gonna say one
of the interesting things

01:05:02.240 --> 01:05:05.280
about the post-1492 cartographic history

01:05:05.280 --> 01:05:07.730
is how quickly the discoveries,

01:05:07.730 --> 01:05:11.190
the knowledge moves
around the world, right.

01:05:11.190 --> 01:05:15.170
Once people have mapped the
correct, the accurate outlines

01:05:15.170 --> 01:05:17.810
or even inaccurate outlines,
that information is spreading

01:05:17.810 --> 01:05:20.090
'cause everybody wants that information.

01:05:20.090 --> 01:05:24.080
- So I had a related question,
Valerie, which is, you know

01:05:24.080 --> 01:05:27.770
what do you think having
studied these earlier voyages?

01:05:27.770 --> 01:05:31.580
What is distinctive about the encounters?

01:05:31.580 --> 01:05:34.070
You know, the colonial encounters that

01:05:34.070 --> 01:05:37.410
happen later on, is it just the scale?

01:05:37.410 --> 01:05:39.950
Is it because, or where there, were these

01:05:39.950 --> 01:05:42.454
peoples when they were moving and trading,

01:05:42.454 --> 01:05:44.660
did they try to conquer
each other already?

01:05:44.660 --> 01:05:46.160
And we just don't know.

01:05:46.160 --> 01:05:49.950
And I'm just wondering, you
know, that colonial impulse

01:05:49.950 --> 01:05:54.800
right, seems to have
really come to the fore

01:05:54.800 --> 01:05:59.210
in the you know, 14,
1500's later on, right?

01:05:59.210 --> 01:06:01.720
And I was curious whether you wondered

01:06:01.720 --> 01:06:03.290
was there something different?

01:06:03.290 --> 01:06:06.320
As you said, maybe is it
because there is information,

01:06:06.320 --> 01:06:09.680
Europe is then by then
information transmission

01:06:09.680 --> 01:06:12.130
within Europe is very rapid

01:06:12.130 --> 01:06:16.360
and things have really shifted
in Europe by then, or...

01:06:17.290 --> 01:06:18.660
- That's part of the story.

01:06:18.660 --> 01:06:21.440
I was gonna say one of the
things that European historians

01:06:21.440 --> 01:06:23.080
talk about which I think
is very interesting

01:06:23.080 --> 01:06:25.440
is the idea of a colony.

01:06:25.440 --> 01:06:29.300
There's a whole bunch of
Chinese historians who would say

01:06:29.300 --> 01:06:31.090
oh, when the Chinese get someplace new

01:06:31.090 --> 01:06:33.610
they don't have this
desire to form a colony,

01:06:33.610 --> 01:06:37.240
and that can be a very
rosy view of the Chinese.

01:06:37.240 --> 01:06:39.620
And they certainly
meddled in local politics

01:06:39.620 --> 01:06:41.020
and even overthrew princes

01:06:41.020 --> 01:06:43.760
and replaced them with
their own candidates.

01:06:43.760 --> 01:06:45.630
But they didn't seem to have an idea

01:06:45.630 --> 01:06:48.567
that you go to someplace new that if it's

01:06:48.567 --> 01:06:51.371
contiguous with China, then
you can add it to China.

01:06:51.371 --> 01:06:53.120
But if you go to someplace,
that's not contiguous

01:06:53.120 --> 01:06:55.930
with China, there seemed to
just like, leave it alone.

01:06:55.930 --> 01:06:57.460
They would like that the local ruler

01:06:57.460 --> 01:06:59.060
to acknowledge the Chinese ruler.

01:06:59.060 --> 01:07:01.610
The Europeans have this model of a colony.

01:07:01.610 --> 01:07:03.505
And, you know, there's
Roman colonies right?

01:07:03.505 --> 01:07:04.690
That goes way back.

01:07:04.690 --> 01:07:06.990
But there is been some
very interesting work about

01:07:06.990 --> 01:07:10.140
the Crusades and the crusader
colonies in the Mediterranean.

01:07:10.140 --> 01:07:12.790
So that's part of the answer
is what's going on in Europe.

01:07:12.790 --> 01:07:17.350
And when the Columbus gets to Hispaniola,

01:07:17.350 --> 01:07:21.060
how he made his first
instinct is to claim it

01:07:21.060 --> 01:07:22.880
for Ferdinand and Isabelle, right.

01:07:22.880 --> 01:07:26.120
He's not where I think the
people I'm talking about,

01:07:26.120 --> 01:07:27.660
they might've landed but I don't think

01:07:27.660 --> 01:07:31.460
their first impulse is to
claim it for any ruler.

01:07:31.460 --> 01:07:33.943
And then I think a key factor

01:07:33.943 --> 01:07:37.340
and especially significant today,

01:07:37.340 --> 01:07:40.600
thinking about COVID is this the role that

01:07:40.600 --> 01:07:45.570
disease played when the
Europeans arrived in the Americas

01:07:45.570 --> 01:07:47.597
and the catastrophic consequences

01:07:47.597 --> 01:07:49.620
it had on the local population.

01:07:49.620 --> 01:07:50.453
- Right.

01:07:50.453 --> 01:07:55.453
So I see a comment on
the chat where, you know,

01:07:55.600 --> 01:07:59.380
colonialism is not an artifact
of European expansion,

01:07:59.380 --> 01:08:01.310
but you know, the Greeks and Phoenicians

01:08:01.310 --> 01:08:03.190
are also European incidentally.

01:08:03.190 --> 01:08:04.778
So they're giving me some--

01:08:04.778 --> 01:08:07.620
- And I mentioned the Romans too, right?

01:08:07.620 --> 01:08:09.730
It's not, yes, I agree.

01:08:09.730 --> 01:08:12.540
Colonialism was not an
artifact of European expansion.

01:08:12.540 --> 01:08:15.094
I think the interesting question
is where does it come from?

01:08:15.094 --> 01:08:17.410
- Has it come from, and
when does it start, right?

01:08:17.410 --> 01:08:21.760
So, because it's a point in
time where we can see this kind

01:08:21.760 --> 01:08:25.170
of notion of a colony

01:08:26.100 --> 01:08:30.090
develop and ripen as it were, right.

01:08:30.090 --> 01:08:32.060
And I think the Crusades
have a lot to do with that.

01:08:32.060 --> 01:08:34.900
I mean, the crusader
states in the Holy Land

01:08:34.900 --> 01:08:39.470
and then when they're forced to withdraw

01:08:39.470 --> 01:08:41.340
when Saladin defeats them,

01:08:41.340 --> 01:08:43.344
and they withdraw to Cyprus, right?

01:08:43.344 --> 01:08:45.830
That argument I find very compelling

01:08:45.830 --> 01:08:48.710
about the idea of colonies going back.

01:08:48.710 --> 01:08:50.840
I mean, there are earlier precedents,

01:08:50.840 --> 01:08:54.730
but that I think you can
draw a continuous line

01:08:54.730 --> 01:08:58.700
from the Crusader colonies
in the Mediterranean to some

01:08:58.700 --> 01:09:02.630
of the colonies in the
Atlantic, like Madeira

01:09:03.500 --> 01:09:07.943
or the Canary islands and
then to the Americans.

01:09:09.340 --> 01:09:10.173
- Thank you.

